VISUALIZATION & DIAGRAMS

Relation as a directed bipartite graph

A binary relation
--- 
𝑅⊆𝐴×𝐵
 can be visualized as arrows from elements of 𝐴 to elements of 𝐵.

A-domain                 B-codomain
   a1   a2   a3   --->   b1   b2   b3

   a1 ---------> b2
   a2 ---------> b1
   a3 ---------> b3

Converse 
---
𝑅^𝑇
Flip all arrows.

Original R:          Converse Rᵀ:
a1 ---> b2           b2 ---> a1
a2 ---> b1           b1 ---> a2
a3 ---> b3           b3 ---> a3

Composition 
---
𝑅⋅𝑆
Follow arrows through an intermediate set.

A --R--> B --S--> C

a1 --R--> b2 --S--> c3
a2 --R--> b1 --S--> c1

Composition R·S:
a1 ---> c3
a2 ---> c1

Kronecker 
---
𝑅⊗𝑆
Parallel application on product domains.

R: A → B        S: C → D

Kronecker R ⊗ S:
(A × C) → (B × D)

(a,c) ---> (b,d)
iff a R b AND c S d

A: a1,a2        C: c1,c2
B: b1,b2        D: d1,d2

(a1,c1) ---> (b2,d1)
(a2,c2) ---> (b1,d2)

Strict Fork 
---
𝑅⋔𝑆
Two relations share the same domain, outputs paired.

A --R--> B
A --S--> C

Strict Fork R ⋔ S:
A ---> (B × C)

a ---> (b,c)
iff a R b AND a S c

Example:
a1 --R--> b2
a1 --S--> c3

Then:
a1 ---> (b2, c3)

Strict Join 
---
𝑅⋎𝑆
Two relations share the same codomain, inputs paired.

A --R--> B <--S-- C

Strict Join R ⋎ S:
(A × C) ---> B

(a,c) ---> b
iff a R b AND c S b

Example:
a2 --R--> b1
c3 --S--> b1

Then:
(a2,c3) ---> b1

Domain and Codomain extraction
---
Domain picks out all “left nodes” with outgoing edges.
R:
a1 ---> b2
a2 ---> b1

dom(R):
a1, a2

Codomain picks out all “right nodes” with incoming edges.
cod(R):
b1, b2

